<header><p class="st-kicker">other · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Paris Commune</h1><p class="st-meta">1871 CE – 1871 CE · capital Paris</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The revolutionary government that ruled Paris for ten weeks in 1871, and whose bloody suppression became a lasting symbol for the socialist left.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France's</a> defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and the long siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolphe-thiers">Adolphe Thiers</a> tried to remove the National Guard's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannons</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montmartre">Montmartre</a> on 18 March 1871. Parisians resisted, the government withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>, and elections on 26 March created the Commune.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Commune's council, a mix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobin-club">Jacobins</a>, socialists and followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon">Proudhon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-auguste-blanqui">Blanqui</a>, separated church and state, abolished night work in bakeries, let workers take over abandoned workshops and postponed rents. Women such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louise-michel">Louise Michel</a> played prominent roles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communards">Communards</a> also pulled down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vendome-column">Vendôme Column</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Government troops entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 21 May, and during the 'Bloody Week' that followed thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communards">Communards</a> were killed, many by summary execution; the Communards shot hostages and burned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuileries-palace">Tuileries</a>. Thousands more were tried and deported to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-caledonia">New Caledonia</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-third-republic">French Third Republic</a> survived.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/paris-commune.jpg" alt="Paris Commune" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barricade18March1871_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1871 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Paris</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis Charles Delescluze</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Commune de Paris</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1871&amp;focus=paris-commune" aria-label="Paris Commune on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4734 472 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Paris Commune lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5054 728L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Paris. <a href="/atlas?year=1871&amp;focus=paris-commune">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

